Z||000000||isthiswhereiputausername?||07-25-2001||07:23 PM||||I recently took my car out of a 3 year storage in a garage, tank was 1/2 full of gas. <P> Yesterday I filled it to little over a 3/4 tank and when it was parked on a slight (nose)down angle, I started to smell gas bad. <BR> I jacked it up and looked underneath and the front floor by the tank (pass side)was wet with gas. <P> Tank never had a leak before and was always undercoated..<P><BR>Is there any chance theres a gasket that dried out or did she rust out? <P>||||reg||9
Z||000001||theogre||07-25-2001||07:56 PM||||It might be the seal for the sender/pump module but most likely it rusted thru someplace. If it's just one or 2 spots it can be reliably repaired otherwise replace it.||||reg||1
Z||000002||isthiswhereiputausername?||07-25-2001||08:31 PM||||Thanks  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">.. Guess I will pull it off next weekend and have a look ||||reg||1
Z||000003||terryk||07-25-2001||10:40 PM||||If the sender o-ring is hosed (not uncommon), use only a GM replacement. These are wire-form with tabs. The aftermarket rings are a b1tch to keep in place will you rotate the lock ring.<P>||||reg||1
Z||000004||Indiana_resto_guy||07-26-2001||11:25 AM||||Check the hoses for dry rot!<BR>If you smelled gas after driving home, it could very well be a rotten fuel supply line. Find one bad, replace all!<P>Hope this helps!<BR>You should drop the tank for this!||||reg||1
Z||000005||terryk||07-26-2001||11:50 AM||||Good point. I thought he checked that since it's metal lines to the rear of the tank. ||||reg||1
Z||000006||isthiswhereiputausername?||07-26-2001||12:41 PM||||Lines are all good. <P>It was stored indoors at a spare bay we had in the garage,  I only noticed the gas fumes/then a wet patch after I filled the tank up past 3/4s<P>||||reg||1
